{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Soqtch7Y8Z2D8NswmQvryR","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ICLR 2025 reviewer guidelines rating scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ICLR 2025 Reviewer Guide\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide\"},{\"title\":\"ICML 2025 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerInstructions\"},{\"title\":\"Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ACGuide\"},{\"title\":\"WHAT DRIVES PAPER ACCEPTANCE? A PROCESS- CENTRIC ANALYSIS OF MODERN PEER REVIEW\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.25701\"},{\"title\":\"Can LLM feedback enhance review quality? A randomized study of 20K   reviews at ICLR 2025\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09737\"},{\"title\":\"ReviewerToo: Should AI Join The Program Committee? A Look At The Future of Peer Review\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.08867\"},{\"title\":\"GUIDE: Towards Scalable Advising for Research Ideas\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.08870\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found a reference to the ICLR 2025 reviewer guidelines, but the specific rating scale details weren't fully displayed in the search results. However, I can see that ICLR uses a rating scale where papers are divided into three groups: High-Score (score > 6), Borderline (5 ≤ score ≤ 6), and Low-Score (score < 5).\n\nThe official ICLR 2025 Reviewer Guide is available at https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide, which would contain the complete details of the rating scale and all reviewer guidelines. You can access that page directly to see the full rating scale definitions and other reviewer instructions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013Kgvyzk6NEUhNd7tsGeanW","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"a84381ef-55c1-4a92-aa7d-fb99edcedb5a","timestamp