{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BANGLVwkU9riro677xCNu3","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ICLR OpenReview reviewer form numerical score\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15462\"},{\"title\":\"What Have We Learned from OpenReview?\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.05885\"},{\"title\":\"Integrating Rankings into Quantized Scores in Peer Review | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=Kb1lb0vSLa\"},{\"title\":\"Paper scoring through individual reviewer quality estimates for Open Peer Review\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.13014v2\"},{\"title\":\"Are numerical scores important for grant assessment? A cross-sectional study\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11362741/\"},{\"title\":\"Integrating Rankings into Quantized Scores in Peer Review\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.03505\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about ICLR OpenReview reviewer form numerical scores:\n\nNumerical scores are provided for soundness, presentation, contribution, confidence, and overall rating.\n\nThe specific scoring scales have varied over time:\n\n- For ICLR 2017-2019, reviewers gave a review score (integer) from 1 to 10, and selected a confidence level (integer) between 1 and 5.\n\n- For ICLR 2020, reviewers gave a rating score in {1, 3, 6, 8} and selected an experience assessment score (similar to confidence score) between 1 and 4.\n\nThe scores are \"usually elicited in a quantized form to accommodate the limited cognitive ability of humans to describe their opinions in numerical values,\" and it has been found that quantized scores \"suffer from a large number of ties, thereby leading to a significant loss of information.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013Kgvyzk6NEUhNd7tsGeanW","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"f45e6af4-7895-435e-8c73-5dad4ddc0cd5","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:20:13.386Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012pS5fLiktwoCoQi8w4Zd8T","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"ICLR\" \"reviewer\" \"soundness\" \"presentation\" \"originality\" \"significance\"\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15462\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397780200_Insights_from_the_ICLR_Peer_Review_and_Rebuttal_Process\"},{\"title\":\"ICML 2026 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerInstructions\"},{\"title\":\"Reviewer Tutorial 2022\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/Conferences/2022/ReviewerTutorial\"},{\"title\":\"ICML 2024 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/Conferences/2024/ReviewerInstructions\"},{\"title\":\"What Makes a Scientific Paper be Accepted for Publication?\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.07112\"},{\"title\":\"Reviewer Guidelines – ECML PKDD 2023\",\"url\":\"https://2023.ecmlpkdd.org/organisation/reviewer-guidelines/\"},{\"title\":\"ICML 2025 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerInstructions\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for your query about ICLR reviewer criteria. Here are the key findings:\n\n## ICLR Review Scoring Criteria\n\nNumerical scores are provided for soundness, presentation, contribution, confidence, and overall rating in ICLR reviews.\n\n## Key Insights from ICLR Peer Review Analysis\n\nThe overall rating correlates most strongly with contribution, then soundness. Additionally, the overall rating shows a positive correlation with the word count of the strengths section and a negative correlation with the word count of the weaknesses section.\n\n## Common Issues Identified by Reviewers\n\nMost frequent issues are overlap with prior work, lack of originality, and lack of clear contribution.\n\n## How These Criteria Are Used in Peer Review\n\nIn experiments with a dataset from ICLR 2017, reviewers focused on aspects such as originality, clarity, impact, and soundness of a paper, when suggesting whether or not the paper should be accepted for publication.\n\nThe search results show that these evaluation criteria (soundness, presentation, originality, and significance) are standard across major machine learning conferences and are central to their peer review processes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013Kgvyzk6NEUhNd7tsGeanW","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"dab38c41-2b79-4765-bcef-1fa3e6e4f930","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:20:14.399Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCTJ6R1D4NrFBUhEyTtf2u","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ICLR reviewer recommendation options accept reject decision\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ICLR 2021 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2021/ReviewerGuide\"},{\"title\":\"Area Chair Guide\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2023/ACGuide\"},{\"title\":\"Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ACGuide\"},{\"title\":\"ICLR 2026 Reviewer Guide\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuide\"},{\"title\":\"ICLR 2026 Area Chair Guide\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/AreaChairGuide\"},{\"title\":\"ICLR 2022 Reviewer Instructions\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide\"},{\"title\":\"A Retrospective on the ICLR 2026 Review Process – ICLR Blog\",\"url\":\"https://blog.iclr.cc/2026/03/31/a-retrospective-on-the-iclr-2026-review-process/\"},{\"title\":\"PaperDecision | Modeling the Complete Peer Review Process\",\"url\":\"https://paperdecision.netlify.app/\"},{\"title\":\"ICLR 2020 Reviewing Guidelines\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2020/ReviewerGuide\"},{\"title\":\"An Open Review of OpenReview: A Critical Analysis of the Machine   Learning Conference Review Process\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05137\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about ICLR reviewer recommendation options:\n\nICLR reviewers are asked to clearly state their initial recommendation as either accept or reject with one or two key reasons for this choice. The guidance indicates that reviewers should clearly state their initial recommendation (accept or reject) with one or two key reasons for this choice.\n\nAdditionally, reviewers can change their reviews after the author response period, and should state their reasoning and what did or didn't change their recommendation throughout the discussion phase.\n\nThe search results show that while the basic recommendation structure involves accept or reject decisions at the reviewer level, the final paper decisions can be more granular. According to one source discussing ICLR 2026, complete accept lists include decisions categorized as Oral, Spotlight, Poster, or Reject. However, these appear to be tiers assigned during the final decision-making process by Area Chairs and Program Chairs, rather than the initial reviewer recommendation options.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013Kgvyzk6NEUhNd7tsGeanW","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"6e0f486f-8f57-4556-b50d-90bbbaba7dc8","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:20:16.745Z"}
