{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Kgvyzk6NEUhNd7tsGeanW","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my research, here's what I found about ICLR reviewer guidelines and scoring:\n\n## Official ICLR Rating Scales\n\nICLR has used different rating scales over time:\n\n**ICLR 2017-2019**: Continuous scale of 1-10 (integer scores)\n\n**ICLR 2020-2023**: Discrete scale {1, 3, 6, 8}\n\n**ICLR 2024-2025**: Discrete scale {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10}\n\n**ICLR 2026**: Discrete scale {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10} or {2, 4, 6, 8, 10} (moves toward forcing more decisive opinions)\n\n## Official Scoring Dimensions\n\nAccording to the search results, ICLR reviewers are asked to provide numerical scores for:\n\n1. **Soundness** - Technical correctness and rigor of the work\n2. **Presentation** - Clarity and quality of writing/presentation\n3. **Contribution** - Originality and significance of the contribution\n4. **Confidence** - Reviewer's confidence in their assessment (typically 1-5 scale)\n5. **Overall Rating** - The final recommendation score\n\n## Recommendation Structure\n\nRather than a traditional numerical rating, ICLR reviewers provide:\n- An **initial recommendation**: Accept or Reject\n- **One or two key reasons** for the recommendation\n- **Structured written feedback** addressing four key questions:\n  - What problem is being tackled?\n  - Is the approach well-motivated?\n  - Do results support the claims?\n  - What is the significance of the work?\n\nThe overall rating correlates most strongly with the contribution score, followed by soundness.\n\n## Paper Decision Tiers\n\nAt the final decision-making stage, Area Chairs and Program Chairs assign papers to: Oral, Spotlight, Poster, or Reject.\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n- [ICLR 2026 Reviewer Guide](https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuide)\n- [ICLR 2025 Reviewer Guide](https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide)\n- [ICLR 2024 Reviewer Guide](https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/ReviewerGuide)\n- [ICLR 2026 Submission Storm: 19,797 Papers, New Review Rules](https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/169/iclr-2026-submission-storm-19-797-papers-new-review-rules-and-the-age-of-ai-convergence)\n- [Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15462)\n- [A Retrospective on the ICLR 2026 Review Process](https://blog.iclr.cc/2026/03/31/a-retrospective-on-the-iclr-2026-review-process/)\n- [Can LLM feedback enhance review quality? A randomized study of 20K reviews at ICLR 2025](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09737)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"2cd2f1b8-6768-4845-a27b-ee2da6051b89","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:20:44.558Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Search the web for \"ICLR 2026 reviewer guidelines scoring criteria\" or \"ICLR 2025 reviewer guidelines rating scale\". I need to find the official ICLR reviewer rating scale (typically 1-10) and what each score means. Also search for \"ICLR reviewer form\" to find the exact categories reviewers must score on (soundness, presentation, contribution, etc.). Report the full rating scale and all scoring dimensions.","agentId":"ac40ea55a5a72b52b","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my research, here's what I found about ICLR reviewer guidelines and scoring:\n\n## Official ICLR Rating Scales\n\nICLR has used different rating scales over time:\n\n**ICLR 2017-2019**: Continuous scale of 1-10 (integer scores)\n\n**ICLR 2020-2023**: Discrete scale {1, 3, 6, 8}\n\n**ICLR 2024-2025**: Discrete scale {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10}\n\n**ICLR 2026**: Discrete scale {0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10} or {2, 4, 6, 8, 10} (moves toward forcing more decisive opinions)\n\n## Official Scoring Dimensions\n\nAccording to the search results, ICLR reviewers are asked to provide numerical scores for:\n\n1. **Soundness** - Technical correctness and rigor of the work\n2. **Presentation** - Clarity and quality of writing/presentation\n3. **Contribution** - Originality and significance of the contribution\n4. **Confidence** - Reviewer's confidence in their assessment (typically 1-5 scale)\n5. **Overall Rating** - The final recommendation score\n\n## Recommendation Structure\n\nRather than a traditional numerical rating, ICLR reviewers provide:\n- An **initial recommendation**: Accept or Reject\n- **One or two key reasons** for the recommendation\n- **Structured written feedback** addressing four key questions:\n  - What problem is being tackled?\n  - Is the approach well-motivated?\n  - Do results support the claims?\n  - What is the significance of the work?\n\nThe overall rating correlates most strongly with the contribution score, followed by soundness.\n\n## Paper Decision Tiers\n\nAt the final decision-making stage, Area Chairs and Program Chairs assign papers to: Oral, Spotlight, Poster, or Reject.\n\n---\n\n**Sources:**\n- [ICLR 2026 Reviewer Guide](https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/ReviewerGuide)\n- [ICLR 2025 Reviewer Guide](https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/ReviewerGuide)\n- [ICLR 2024 Reviewer Guide](https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2024/ReviewerGuide)\n- [ICLR 2026 Submission Storm: 19,797 Papers, New Review Rules](https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/169/iclr-2026-submission-storm-19-797-papers-new-review-rules-and-the-age-of-ai-convergence)\n- [Insights from the ICLR Peer Review and Rebuttal Process](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15462)\n- [A Retrospective on the ICLR 2026 Review Process](https://blog.iclr.cc/2026/03/31/a-retrospective-on-the-iclr-2026-review-process/)\n- [Can LLM feedback enhance review quality? A randomized study of 20K reviews at ICLR 2025](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09737)"}],"totalDurationMs":53277,"totalTokens":26054,"totalToolUseCount":14,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1675,"cache_read_input_tokens":23593,"output_tokens":780,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1675},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":780,"cache_read_input_tokens":23593,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1675,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1675,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":14}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018AxZ4PRDjAKwitFHcYebwL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ICLR scoring scale. Let me use the ICLR 2025 scale which seems most reliable: {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10}. Let me now dispatch the two reviewers in parallel.\n\nActually, let me use what I can confirm. The search results suggest ICLR uses a 1-10 scale with specific scoring dimensions: Soundness, Presentation, Contribution, Overall Rating, and Confidence. Let me use the standard ICLR format.\n\nFor ICLR 2025, the scale is typically:\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n\n\n- 8: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold  \n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1: Strong reject\n\nNow I'll set up two specialized reviewers working in parallel—one focusing on the technical soundness and experimental validation, the other examining the novelty and positioning relative to existing work—then craft targeted prompts for each.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1837,"cache_read_input_tokens":51426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1837},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"d637c0cb-d752-44d8-b352-762ca9489ca8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018AxZ4PRDjAKwitFHcYebwL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ICLR 评分标准已获取。现在并行派遣两位 Reviewer 进行独立审稿 (Step 1)。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1837,"cache_read_input_tokens":51426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1837},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"343248c2-7ac3-4f0c-bbaa-894dc9cbab72"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018AxZ4PRDjAKwitFHcYebwL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NyCgoFNWhKJm8WzxRQYWCx","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** performing an independent ICLR review of a mechanistic interpretability paper. Your focus areas are **methods** and **experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Cross-Lingual Knowledge Conflict Signals in Multilingual LLMs: Directional Asymmetry, Layer Structure, and Causal Probing\"\n\nThe paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are encoded in residual stream representations of multilingual LLMs. It uses linear probes across three architectures (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) and two language pairs (EN↔ZH, EN↔JA).\n\n**Key Claims:**\n- C1: Conflict signals are linearly detectable in residual streams (balanced accuracy 69–77%)\n- C2: Directional asymmetry ZH→EN > EN→ZH is remarkably consistent across architectures (pooled effect 6.6±0.9pp, I²=0%)\n- C4: Conflict detection peaks during the language-universal-to-specific CKA transition window (50–55% depth)\n- Representation steering yields 18–34% flip rates vs 6% random control (Cohen's d=0.41)\n- Preliminary SAE analysis (Appendix B) is inconclusive due to base-instruct mismatch\n\n**Methods (Section 3):**\n- 3.1 Data Construction: ConflictQA dataset, translated to ZH and JA via neural MT, verified by back-translation COMET scores. EN↔ZH n≈1000 (expanded to ~1800 with ParaRel-conflict subset). EN↔JA n≈680.\n- 3.2 Probing Framework: Logistic regression (L2-regularized) on residual-stream activations at every layer. Four transfer configurations (EN→EN, ZH→ZH, EN→ZH, ZH→EN). 100-iteration bootstrap CIs, permutation tests (n=10,000).\n- 3.3 Confound Exclusion Pipeline: Token overlap control (Jaccard similarity), COMET translation quality ablation, label noise robustness (5-20% injection).\n- 3.4 Layer-wise CKA/SVCCA analysis between EN and ZH conflict activations at each layer.\n- 3.5 Representation Steering: Probe weight vectors as steering directions (α·w_probe added to residual-stream activations). Measures flip rate + coherence preservation. Random orthogonal direction as control.\n- 3.6 Preliminary SAE Analysis: Qwen-Scope pretrained SAEs (65,536 features, trained on Qwen3-8B-Base) applied to instruct model. MI > 0.15 feature selection. LangFIR random-token filtering.\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- 4.1 (C1): Dataset expansion from n≈1000 to n≈1800 tightens CI from [1.1, 13.6] to [2.3, 11.7] while preserving 7.0pp effect. Within-language balanced accuracy 69.4–77.1%.\n- 4.2 (C2): Gemma-2-9B-IT shows same asymmetry direction but gap 5.4pp (CI [-0.8, 11.6], not significant). Cross-architecture: mean 6.6±1.04pp, Cochran's Q p=0.68, I²=0%. Only 1/3 models reaches significance.\n- 4.3 EN↔JA: Same direction (JA→EN 65.8% > EN→JA 61.2%), gap 4.6pp, CI [-2.1, 11.3], not significant. n=680.\n- 4.4 (C4): CKA decreases from 0.92 (early) to 0.63 (final). Probe peaks at 60-85% depth. CKA-probe correlation r=-0.67. Transition onset 50-55%. But probe peak varies 67-88% across models.\n- 4.5 PCA: Low silhouette (0.31), only 23% variance explained by first 2 PCs. Qualitative patterns: EN-ZH overlap decreases from 0.78 to 0.41 across layers.\n- 4.6 Steering: ZH→EN direction flip rate 34.2% vs random 5.8%. EN→ZH direction 18.1% vs random 6.2%. Cohen's d=0.41. Optimal α=3.5. Explicitly framed as correlational, not causal.\n- Appendix A: Label noise robustness — 4% pipeline error rate causes only ~1pp accuracy loss.\n- Appendix B: SAE analysis — base-instruct cosine similarity 0.71, only 35% features interpretable, Jaccard near-zero (0.04-0.06), all inconclusive.\n\n**Imagined experiments from the review context (to check coverage):**\nThe paper was designed to include these experiments. Check which are covered and how well:\n1. Third architecture (Gemma) ✓ — Section 4.2\n2. EN↔JA extension ✓ — Section 4.3\n3. Layer-wise CKA/SVCCA ✓ — Section 4.4\n4. Representation steering ✓ — Section 4.6\n5. PCA visualization ✓ — Section 4.5\n6. Label noise ablation ✓ — Appendix A\n7. Dataset expansion ✓ — Section 4.1\n8. Qwen-Scope SAE ✓ — Appendix B.1\n9. LangFIR decomposition ✓ — Appendix B.2\n10. Cross-lingual Jaccard ✓ — Appendix B.3\n11-13. Track 3 (SAE-based steering) — Honestly reported as deferred\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **methods and experiments**. Address:\n\n1. **Soundness of methods**: Are the probing, statistical testing, confound exclusion, CKA analysis, and steering methods appropriate and correctly applied?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Sample sizes, statistical power, bootstrap/permutation testing adequacy, effect sizes.\n3. **Key weakness**: Only 1/3 models reaches significance for the asymmetry claim (C2). The paper reframes this using Cumming (2014) \"new statistics\" — is this framing legitimate or spinning?\n4. **Confound control**: Is the token overlap / COMET ablation / label noise pipeline sufficient? Any missed confounds?\n5. **Steering experiment quality**: Is the probe-weight steering method well-controlled? Are flip rates meaningful?\n6. **SAE analysis limitations**: Is the honest reporting of base-instruct mismatch adequate, or should this have been addressed before publication?\n7. **Missing experiments or controls** that would significantly strengthen the claims.\n8. **Reproducibility**: Are there enough details for reproduction?\n\n## ICLR Rating Scale\nUse the ICLR scale: {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10}\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 8: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1: Strong reject\n\nProvide scores for: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-10 scale above), Confidence (1-5).\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths\n### Weaknesses\n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments/Controls\n### Scores\n### Recommendation"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1837,"cache_read_input_tokens":51426,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1837},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"751dfb43-f42a-4e7f-95f1-1440fe309e26"}
