T ablation, label noise robustness)\n3. Mechanistic analysis: CKA linking conflict peaks to language transition window + probe-based steering\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Knowledge conflicts in LLMs: Longpre et al. 2021, Chen et al. 2022, Xie et al. 2024 (ConflictQA), CLEAR (Zhao et al. 2026)\n- Cross-lingual representations: Pires et al. 2019, Chi et al. 2020, Wendler et al. 2024 (\"Do llamas work in English?\")\n- Mechanistic interpretability: Belinkov 2022 (probing), Bricken et al. 2023, Cunningham et al. 2023 (SAEs), SpARE (Wu et al. 2025)\n- Representation steering: Turner et al. 2023, Marks & Tegmark 2024\n\n**Limitations stated by authors:**\n1. Two language pairs only (EN↔ZH, EN↔JA)\n2. ConflictQA only — entity-centric factual conflicts only\n3. Steering is correlational, not causal\n4. SAE analysis inconclusive\n5. All models 8-9B parameters\n\n**Result quality notes from review context (which you should treat as ground truth about the experimental design):**\n- The asymmetry (C2) is the core finding but only 1/3 models reaches conventional significance\n- The paper reframes using Cumming (2014) \"new statistics\" — effect-size stability over p-values\n- CKA analysis connects to Wendler et al. 2024 language transition literature\n- Probe peak varies 67-88% across models, weakening \"universal pattern\" claims\n- SAE Track 2 produced noisy results due to base-instruct mismatch (cosine similarity 0.71)\n- Track 3 entirely deferred\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **novelty, significance, and related work positioning**. Address:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is \"first mechanistic account of cross-lingual conflict encoding\" a genuine contribution? How does it advance beyond CLEAR and existing cross-lingual representation work?\n2. **Significance of findings**: \n   - Is ~75% balanced accuracy for conflict detection (C1) scientifically interesting or expected?\n   - Is 6.6pp asymmetry (only 1/3 significant) a noteworthy finding?\n   - Does the CKA-probe peak connection (C4) add to Wendler et al. 2024?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing important references? Consider:\n   - Other cross-lingual probing work\n   - Knowledge editing literature\n   - Multilingual mechanistic interpretability work\n   - Other probe-transfer studies\n4. **Claim calibration**: Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence? Is the paper overclaiming or underclaiming?\n5. **Impact potential**: Would this paper meaningfully advance the field? Who would cite it and why?\n6. **Framing quality**: Is the H1-vs-H2 framing productive? Does the evidence actually discriminate between them?\n7. **Track 2/3 handling**: Is relegating inconclusive SAE results to appendix and deferring Track 3 acceptable for ICLR, or does it leave too much unfinished?\n\n## ICLR Rating Scale\nUse the ICLR scale: {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10}\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 8: Top 15%, strong accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 1: Strong reject\n\nProvide scores for: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-10 scale above), Confidence (1-5).\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths\n### Weaknesses\n### Questions for Authors\n### Positioning and Missing References\n### Scores\n### Recommendation"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F4qgMk8uE7Lx2WdRXGpbNh","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"58eb3635-19c1-4ca2-bf77-cbc58349fb34","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:22:03.406Z"}
