{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a5e623baab8b0d70a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01F4qgMk8uE7Lx2WdRXGpbNh","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":65434,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":167334},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"2c188a09-118b-4cea-a2a4-919c0985b9a6","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VPRquJPKnBB2mnw7Dv2Cuy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QW3YseqGjsyVnAoym8ekp9","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"clkc_mech_interp","tier":"median","dream_id":"dream_20260522T023801","overall_score":"5 — Marginally below acceptance threshold","report":"---\nreviewer: R2\nfocus: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\nvenue: ICLR 2027\npaper: \"Cross-Lingual Knowledge Conflict Signals in Multilingual LLMs: Directional Asymmetry, Layer Structure, and Causal Probing\"\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates how cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals are encoded in the residual stream of multilingual LLMs, using linear probes, CKA/SVCCA analysis, and representation steering across three 8-9B parameter models (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B-IT) and two language pairs (EN-ZH, EN-JA). The paper formulates competing hypotheses (H1: English-anchored universal detection vs. H2: language-pair-specific encoding) and reports four main findings: (C1) conflict signals are linearly detectable at 69-77% balanced accuracy; (C2) cross-lingual probe transfer shows a directional asymmetry (X->EN > EN->X) with pooled effect 6.6+/-0.9pp and I^2=0%; (C4) conflict probe peaks coincide with the CKA language-transition window; and steering results (Cohen's d=0.41) provide correlational causal evidence. SAE analysis (Track 2) is reported as inconclusive in the appendix due to base-instruct mismatch, and SAE-based steering (Track 3) is deferred entirely.\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Genuine gap identification.** The paper correctly identifies that CLEAR (Zhao et al., 2026) established a behavioral phenomenon (linguistic affinity governs entity-centric conflict resolution) without providing any mechanistic account. Asking \"how are these signals encoded internally?\" is a well-motivated question that sits at a productive intersection of mechanistic interpretability and multilingual NLP. The question itself is novel even if the answers turn out to be incremental.\n\n**S2. Hypothesis-driven structure is exemplary for interpretability work.** The H1-vs-H2 framing with falsifiable predictions (mid-layer > late-layer feature sharing; directional consistency across language pairs) is a clear methodological strength. Too much interpretability research is exploratory post-hoc pattern-finding; this paper commits to predictions before presenting results. The \"new statistics\" framing (Cumming, 2014) — emphasizing effect-size stability over individual p-values — is appropriately applied to the 3-model comparison where only 1/3 reaches conventional significance.\n\n**S3. Cross-architecture effect-size consistency.** The pooled asymmetry of 6.6+/-0.9pp with I^2=0% across three architectures from two design families is the paper's most compelling empirical result. The coefficient of variation below 15% for the gap across Llama (7.2pp), Qwen (7.2pp), and Gemma (5.4pp) is genuinely noteworthy. This consistency is difficult to explain as an artifact.\n\n**S4. Confound-exclusion methodology is thorough.** The token-overlap analysis (per-sample Jaccard 3-4%, with Llama showing *negative* correlation r=-0.15), COMET tercile ablation (reversed relationship between quality and accuracy), and label-noise robustness testing together form a credible confound-exclusion pipeline. The COMET result in particular — high-quality translations yielding *lower* probe accuracy — is informative and non-obvious.\n\n**S5. Honest reporting of negative/inconclusive results.** The SAE Track 2 results are reported transparently in the appendix with clear identification of the base-instruct mismatch as the root cause (cosine similarity 0.71, 49% conflict-language feature overlap after LangFIR). This is commendable scientific practice, particularly in a field where negative results are frequently suppressed.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The \"first mechanistic account\" novelty claim is overstated relative to the evidence delivered.** The paper positions itself as providing the \"first mechanistic account of cross-lingual conflict encoding,\" but what it actually delivers is: (a) a linear probing result with moderate accuracy, (b) a CKA analysis that confirms what Wendler et al. (2024) already showed about language transition, and (c) correlational steering with modest effect size. The SAE analysis — which would have been the genuinely novel mechanistic decomposition — is inconclusive, and SAE-based steering is entirely absent. What remains is primarily a *probing* paper with a CKA analysis, not a full mechanistic account. The contribution would be more accurately framed as \"first cross-lingual probing study of knowledge conflict signals with preliminary causal evidence.\"\n\n**W2 (Major). The core asymmetry finding (C2) may not be conflict-specific.** English-dominant pretraining creates a general representational asymmetry where English occupies a more central position in the representation space. The X->EN > EN->X transfer pattern could hold for *any* semantic probing task (sentiment, entailment, topic), not just conflict detection. Without a non-conflict control task establishing that the asymmetry is specific to conflict encoding (or at least quantitatively different), the paper cannot distinguish between:\n- (a) Conflict detection is specifically English-anchored (the paper's mechanistic claim)\n- (b) All cross-lingual semantic transfer is English-anchored (a known property of multilingual models, cf. Pires et al. 2019, Conneau et al. 2020)\n\nIf (b) is true, the asymmetry finding reduces from \"novel mechanistic insight about conflict\" to \"replication of known cross-lingual transfer bias on a new task.\" This is the single largest threat to the paper's novelty claim.\n\n**W3 (Major). CKA analysis (C4) adds marginally to Wendler et al. (2024).** The language-transition finding — that EN-ZH CKA peaks in mid-layers and drops in late layers — is well-established by Wendler et al. (2024) and others. The paper's contribution is connecting conflict probe peaks to this transition window, but the connection is weak: probe peak depths vary from 67% to 88% across models, while the transition onset is at 50-55%. A 20-33 percentage point gap between transition onset and probe peak, with substantial model-to-model variation, undermines the claim of a tight mechanistic coupling. The correlation r=-0.67 is computed on 3 data points and is statistically meaningless (the paper itself acknowledges this elsewhere but still reports it). What exactly does it mean for conflict to \"peak during the transition\"? The transition window is broad enough that many phenomena could coincidentally peak within it.\n\n**W4 (Major). H1-vs-H2 is not meaningfully resolved.** The paper claims to favor H1, but:\n- The asymmetry direction alone cannot distinguish H1 from \"English-dominant pretraining creates general transfer bias\" (W2)\n- The SAE Jaccard analysis that would directly test mid-layer vs. late-layer feature sharing is inconclusive (Appendix, p=0.38)\n- Both language pairs tested (EN-ZH, EN-JA) involve CJK languages — testing H2 requires typologically diverse pairs including at least one Indo-European pair\n- The probe peak variation (67-88%) contradicts the \"universal\" aspect of H1\n\nThe evidence is consistent with H1 but does not discriminate it from simpler alternatives. The honest framing would be: \"Our results are consistent with H1 but do not rule out simpler explanations; the critical SAE-based test was inconclusive.\"\n\n**W5 (Moderate). ~75% balanced accuracy for conflict detection is underwhelming and insufficiently contextualized.** Prior monolingual conflict probing achieves ~90%+ accuracy (Zhao et al. 2024 at NeurIPS FMI Workshop; Boer et al. 2026 at 0.96 Macro-F1). The paper does not adequately explain the 15-20 percentage point gap. Possible explanations include: (a) cross-lingual setting is inherently harder, (b) ConflictQA is noisier than the datasets used in prior work, (c) the verification pipeline introduces systematic bias, or (d) the signal is genuinely weaker. The lack of a within-paper monolingual ceiling comparison (training and testing on EN with the exact same pipeline) makes it impossible to attribute the gap. If the within-language accuracy represents the ceiling, then 23-30% of the signal is not linearly decodable, which limits the scope of claims about \"how conflict is encoded.\"\n\n**W6 (Moderate). Steering evidence is correlational, honestly acknowledged but still a gap.** Cohen's d=0.41 is a medium effect, and 18-34% flip rates mean 66-82% of samples are unaffected. The paper correctly labels this as \"correlational evidence consistent with a causal role\" rather than claiming causation. However, for ICLR, where causal mechanistic claims carry weight, this honest hedging also limits the contribution. The paper acknowledges that activation patching (Vig et al. 2020) or distributed alignment search (Geiger et al. 2024) would be needed for definitive causal claims but leaves them to future work. This makes the steering track more of a preliminary pilot than a full contribution.\n\n**W7 (Moderate). Track 2 and Track 3 incompleteness leaves the three-track design hollow.** The paper's narrative centers on \"three complementary analysis tracks,\" but Track 2 produced inconclusive results (appendix) and Track 3 was never attempted. This means the paper is really a one-track paper (probing + CKA) with correlational steering added. The three-track framing in the introduction sets expectations the paper cannot meet. At ICLR, reviewers will note the gap between the promised \"detection -> decomposition -> intervention\" pipeline and the actual \"detection + preliminary correlational intervention\" delivery.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1 (Critical).** Have you tested whether the X->EN > EN->X asymmetry appears for non-conflict semantic tasks (e.g., probing for entity type, sentiment, or factual correctness without conflict)? If the same asymmetry pattern holds at comparable magnitude, how would you revise the \"conflict-specific English-anchored encoding\" claim?\n\n**Q2.** The probe peak varies from 67% depth (Qwen) to 88% depth (Llama). How do you reconcile this 21-percentage-point variation with the claim that conflict signals interact with a \"universal\" language-transition window at 50-55%?\n\n**Q3.** For the EN-JA extension (n=680), the asymmetry gap is 4.6pp with CI [-2.1, 11.3]. This does not reach significance, and the CI is wide. With only 1/4 model-language-pair combinations reaching significance for EN-JA, how confident are you that the JA results support H1 rather than being noise?\n\n**Q4.** The base-instruct cosine similarity of 0.71 is reported as causing Track 2 failure. Have you considered running probing on the base model (matching Track 2) to obtain at least one clean cross-track comparison? What would you expect the probe accuracy to be on base models?\n\n**Q5.** Boer et al. (2026) achieve 0.96 Macro-F1 on monolingual conflict probing. Your within-language ceiling is 77%. Can you decompose this gap into contributions from: (a) dataset differences, (b) model differences, (c) cross-lingual noise in your pipeline, and (d) genuine difficulty of the ConflictQA conflict signal?\n\n**Q6.** The steering coherence at 74% (ZH->EN direction, alpha=3.5) means roughly 1 in 4 steered outputs are incoherent. How was \"coherence\" measured — human evaluation, automated metric, or perplexity-based?\n\n### Positioning and Missing References\n\nThe related work section covers the essential areas but has significant gaps:\n\n**Missing critical references:**\n\n1. **Conneau et al. (2020), \"Emerging Cross-lingual Structure in Pretrained Language Models\" (ACL 2020)** — Foundational work on cross-lingual representation transfer in multilingual models. Essential context for the asymmetry finding.\n\n2. **Muller et al. (2021), \"First Align, then Predict: Understanding the Cross-Lingual Ability of Multilingual BERT\" (EACL 2021)** — Directly relevant to understanding why English serves as an anchor in multilingual representation spaces.\n\n3. **Zhao et al. (2024), \"Analysing the Residual Stream of Language Models Under Knowledge Conflicts\" (NeurIPS 2024 FMI Workshop)** — The direct monolingual predecessor to this work's Track 1, achieving ~90% accuracy. Must be cited and compared to contextualize the 75% cross-lingual result.\n\n4. **Boer et al. (2026), \"Probing for Knowledge Attribution in Large Language Models\"** — 0.96 Macro-F1 monolingual conflict probing on Llama/Mistral/Qwen. The most relevant upper-bound comparison for Track 1.\n\n5. **Pham et al. (2026), \"Where Knowledge Collides\"** — Activation patching + CMAP for intra-memory conflicts in Qwen3-4B. Closest methodological parallel for within-parametric conflict analysis; the paper's steering track would benefit from comparing approaches.\n\n6. **Kialy et al. (2026), \"A Universal Vibe?\"** — First cross-lingual SAE feature discovery for register features + zero-shot steering transfer. Directly relevant to Track 2's cross-lingual SAE analysis and the question of whether cross-lingual feature sharing extends to conflict.\n\n7. **Wong et al. (2026), \"LangFIR\"** — The paper uses LangFIR-style filtering but does not cite the original work (only mentioned in methods without proper citation).\n\n8. **Wang et al. (2025), \"Lost in Multilinguality\" (ACL 2025)** — Cross-lingual factual inconsistency analysis with mechanistic components; directly adjacent work.\n\n9. **Knowledge editing literature** (Meng et al. 2022, Mitchell et al. 2022) — The paper studies how conflict signals are encoded in representations but does not engage with the knowledge editing literature, which addresses the closely related question of where and how factual knowledge is stored and can be modified. The overlap is significant.\n\n10. **Raghu et al. (2017)** — The median paper uses SVCCA but the original SVCCA paper must be cited.\n\nThe bibliography of 18 references (median version) is thin for ICLR. A paper at this intersection should cite 35-50 references spanning cross-lingual probing, knowledge conflicts, mechanistic interpretability, representation similarity, and multilingual factual consistency.\n\n**Positioning issue:** The paper positions against CLEAR but does not engage with the broader \"English as lingua franca of multilingual models\" literature (Wendler et al. 2024 is cited but the connection is superficial; Muller et al. 2021, Blevins & Zettlemoyer 2022, and others have documented English-centricity extensively). The asymmetry finding may be a special case of a well-known phenomenon rather than a conflict-specific discovery.\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 | Methodology is generally appropriate for Track 1 (probing + confound controls), but the missing non-conflict baseline (W2) creates fundamental interpretive ambiguity. The SAE track is inconclusive. Steering is honestly reported as correlational but limited. The r=-0.67 on 3 points should not be reported as a finding. Bootstrap n=100 is below standard. |\n| **Presentation** | 3/4 | Well-organized with clear hypothesis-driven structure. The H1-vs-H2 framing is effective. Confound controls are well-presented. However, the three-track narrative overpromises given that only Track 1 delivers results. The honest SAE appendix is appreciated but highlights the gap between framing and delivery. |\n| **Contribution** | 2/4 | The question is novel and well-motivated. However, the delivered evidence — moderate-accuracy probing, CKA confirmation of known language-transition patterns, and correlational steering — is incremental. Without the non-conflict baseline, the asymmetry may not be conflict-specific. Without functioning SAE analysis, there is no mechanistic decomposition. The confound-exclusion pipeline (contribution #2) is a genuine methodological contribution but is supporting rather than central. |\n| **Overall** | 5 | Marginally below acceptance threshold. The research question is strong and the experimental design is sound in principle, but the execution leaves too many critical questions unresolved: Is the asymmetry conflict-specific? Can the SAE decomposition work with matched distributions? Does the pattern hold for non-CJK languages? The paper reads as a thorough pilot study that identifies a promising signal but cannot yet establish its novelty relative to known cross-lingual transfer asymmetries. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I am confident in this assessment. My expertise spans cross-lingual representation learning and mechanistic interpretability. The main uncertainty is whether the non-conflict baseline would actually show a different pattern (Q1) — if it does, the paper's contribution would be significantly stronger. |\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Score: 5 — Marginally below acceptance threshold.**\n\nThe paper asks the right question and applies sound methodology, but falls short of the ICLR bar for three interconnected reasons:\n\n1. **Novelty is underdetermined.** The core asymmetry finding (C2) cannot be attributed to conflict-specific encoding without a non-conflict baseline task. If the asymmetry is a general property of cross-lingual transfer in English-dominant models, the mechanistic novelty collapses.\n\n2. **The three-track design is only one-third realized.** Track 2 is inconclusive, Track 3 is absent. The paper's strongest potential contribution — SAE-based mechanistic decomposition of cross-lingual conflict features — is precisely what it cannot deliver. What remains is a probing study with supplementary CKA analysis, which is a thinner contribution than the framing suggests.\n\n3. **Language diversity is insufficient for \"universal\" claims.** Two CJK language pairs cannot support claims about English-anchored \"universal\" detection. The linguistic diversity of the evaluation must match the generality of the claims.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** The paper would likely cross the ICLR threshold if the authors: (a) add a non-conflict semantic baseline showing the asymmetry is conflict-specific or at least quantitatively different; (b) include at least one non-CJK language pair (EN-DE or EN-FR); (c) either fix the SAE base-instruct mismatch or remove the three-track framing and present honestly as a probing paper. Any two of these three would likely suffice.\n\n**Who would cite this paper?** In its current form, researchers working on cross-lingual probing would cite the confound-exclusion methodology; researchers studying knowledge conflicts would cite the asymmetry finding with the caveat that conflict-specificity is unestablished. The potential citation impact is moderate but would increase substantially if the non-conflict baseline validates the conflict-specific claim."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":735,"cache_read_input_tokens":64280,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":735,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F4qgMk8uE7Lx2WdRXGpbNh","session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"503e817d-ac1b-42fa-8ddf-714391e067b0"}
