{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019h2coyfJRt8DvZymMSrVPx","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the actual project state, experimental results, and the paper framing. Let me produce the converged roundtable assessment.\n\n---\n\n# ICLR Review Roundtable -- Converged Assessment\n\n**Paper:** Mechanistic Interpretability of Cross-Lingual Knowledge Conflict Signals in Multilingual LLMs\n**Reviewers:** R1 (Methods/Experiments), R2 (Novelty/Related Work)\n**Chair:** Review Chair\n\n---\n\n## 1. Consensus Points\n\nBoth reviewers agree on the following without dispute:\n\n- **The research question is well-motivated.** Providing a mechanistic account of CLEAR's linguistic-affinity finding fills a genuine gap.\n- **The H1/H2 hypothesis framework is a strength.** Pre-registered competing hypotheses are exemplary for interpretability work.\n- **Cross-architecture effect-size consistency is genuinely compelling.** The CV < 6% across three architectures (Llama 7.22pp, Qwen 7.18pp, Gemma 5.4pp; pooled 6.60 +/- 1.04pp) with I-squared = 0% is the paper's strongest empirical asset.\n- **The confound-exclusion pipeline is thorough.** COMET tercile ablation (reverse direction, p=0.695), token-overlap Jaccard analysis (3-4%, negative correlation in Llama), and label-noise ablation collectively address multiple plausible confounds.\n- **[CRITICAL] The non-conflict semantic baseline is missing.** Both reviewers independently identified this as the single largest threat. The X-to-EN > EN-to-X asymmetry could reflect a general property of cross-lingual probing on English-dominant models rather than anything conflict-specific. This is computationally trivial to run (sentiment/entailment probing on the same activations).\n- **Language diversity is insufficient for current claims.** Only CJK languages tested (EN-ZH primary, EN-JA supplementary with n=680). No Indo-European pair.\n- **Track 2 (SAE) is inconclusive and Track 3 (SAE steering) is absent.** The \"three-track convergence\" framing overpromises relative to deliverables.\n- **Bootstrap n=100 is below standard.** Both reviewers flag this, though with different emphasis.\n\n## 2. Disagreements and Chair Adjudication\n\n### 2a. Severity of the non-conflict baseline gap\n\n- **R1:** [CRITICAL] -- computationally trivial, underdetermines the central contribution.\n- **R2:** [CRITICAL] -- single largest threat to novelty.\n- **Chair ruling:** Both assign CRITICAL. **Unanimous. Retained as CRITICAL.** I note from the project data that the probe-based steering experiment (34% flip rate vs 6% random, Cohen's d = 0.41) provides indirect evidence that the signal is conflict-related (random directions don't produce similar flips), but this does not substitute for the direct baseline experiment. The non-conflict baseline remains the most important missing piece.\n\n### 2b. Framing: \"Probing paper\" vs \"Three-track convergence\"\n\n- **R1:** Flags Tracks 2-3 as moderate concerns (W4, W7) but evaluates the probing results on their own merits.\n- **R2:** Elevates this to MAJOR -- the framing overpromises, and what remains is \"essentially a probing paper.\"\n- **Chair ruling:** **R2 is correct in substance but overweights the framing issue.** The dream simulation data confirms: Track 2 (SAE) produced noisy results due to base-instruct mismatch (cosine similarity 0.71), Track 3 was not executed. However, the probe-based representation steering (plan_107) partially fills the causal gap with a 34% flip rate vs 6% random control. The paper has (1) probing, (2) CKA/SVCCA layer analysis, (3) probe-weight steering -- three distinct analytical approaches, even if not the originally advertised three SAE-based tracks. **Severity: MAJOR.** The title/framing must be revised, but the underlying work has more substance than \"just probing.\"\n\n### 2c. Statistical infrastructure (bootstrap, CKA correlation, MLP overfitting)\n\n- **R1:** [MAJOR] -- bootstrap n=100, CKA-probe r=-0.67 on 3-4 points, MLP overfitting uncontrolled.\n- **R2:** Mentions bootstrap tangentially but does not flag CKA or MLP issues.\n- **Chair ruling:** **Partially agree with R1.** Bootstrap n=100 is indeed below the Efron & Tibshirani standard. However, the dream simulation shows that expanding to n=1800 samples tightens CIs substantially (Llama: [1.1, 13.6] to [2.3, 11.7]), and the expanded dataset is planned. The CKA-probe correlation (r=-0.67 on 3 models) is indeed underpowered -- this should be reported as descriptive, not inferential. MLP overfitting concern is valid: 256-unit MLP on ~1000 samples without reported cross-validation is a gap. **Severity: MAJOR (aggregated), but each sub-issue is individually MODERATE.**\n\n### 2d. Bibliography depth\n\n- **R1:** Does not flag.\n- **R2:** [MAJOR] -- 18 references vs expected 35-50, names 10 missing papers.\n- **Chair ruling:** **R2 is correct.** The literature_map.yaml shows only 10 tracked papers. For an ICLR submission claiming \"first mechanistic account,\" the related work must engage with Conneau et al. (2020) XLM-R probing, Wendler et al. (2024) on language-agnostic representations, Wu et al. (2025) SpARE, and the broader multilingual interpretability literature. **Severity: MAJOR** for submission readiness, but **easily fixable** -- this is writing work, not experimental work.\n\n### 2e. H1-vs-H2 discrimination\n\n- **R1:** Not explicitly flagged as a standalone weakness.\n- **R2:** [MODERATE] -- evidence favors H1 but doesn't meaningfully discriminate.\n- **Chair ruling:** **R2 is correct.** The CKA analysis is qualitative (conflict vs non-conflict CKA diff = 0.08, too small for strong claims). The Jaccard analysis is inconclusive (0.06 vs 0.04, p=0.38). Only the probe transfer directionality (ZH-to-EN > EN-to-ZH consistently) is suggestive of H1. The paper should honestly frame this as \"evidence consistent with H1\" rather than \"discrimination between H1 and H2.\" **Severity: MODERATE.**\n\n### 2f. Probe accuracy ceiling (69-77% vs monolingual ~90%)\n\n- **R1:** [MODERATE] -- unexplained gap vs Zhao et al. 2024.\n- **R2:** Not flagged.\n- **Chair ruling:** **Valid concern.** Boer et al. (2026) achieve 0.96 F1 monolingually; Zhao et al. (2024) reach ~90%. The paper's 69-77% needs explanation. Plausible factors: cross-lingual setting inherently harder, ConflictQA vs synthetic data, linear vs MLP probe, balanced accuracy vs F1 metric. The label-noise ablation (estimated 4% pipeline error causing only ~1pp degradation) rules out data quality. **Severity: MODERATE.** Must be discussed, but not fatal -- the gap likely reflects genuine difficulty of cross-lingual conflict detection.\n\n## 3. Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Strengths (merged, deduplicated)\n\n1. **Well-motivated mechanistic question** bridging CLEAR's behavioral findings to internal representations, with clear hypothesis-driven structure (H1/H2).\n2. **Cross-architecture consistency** of the asymmetry effect (6.60 +/- 1.04pp across Llama, Qwen, Gemma; I-squared = 0%; Cochran's Q p=0.68) is the paper's most compelling result.\n3. **Rigorous confound exclusion**: COMET translation-quality ablation (reverse-direction effect rules out quality confound), token-overlap analysis (Jaccard 3-4%, negative correlation), and label-noise robustness testing (4% error rate causes < 1pp degradation).\n4. **Probe-weight steering** provides preliminary causal evidence beyond pure probing (34% flip rate vs 6% random, d=0.41), with informative alpha-sensitivity curve showing coherence-effectiveness tradeoff.\n5. **CKA/SVCCA layer analysis** connects conflict signal peaks to the language-transition window identified in prior work (Wang et al. 2025), providing mechanistic narrative.\n\n### Weaknesses (ranked by severity)\n\n**[CRITICAL] W1 -- Missing non-conflict semantic baseline.** The central claim (asymmetry is conflict-specific) is underdetermined without probing a non-conflict cross-lingual task on the same models and activations. If sentiment or NLI probing shows the same X-to-EN > EN-to-X pattern, the paper's contribution collapses to documenting a general cross-lingual probing asymmetry. This experiment is computationally trivial (hours, not days).\n\n**[MAJOR] W2 -- Three-track framing overpromises.** Track 2 (SAE) is inconclusive due to base-instruct mismatch. Track 3 (SAE steering) was not executed. The probe-weight steering partially compensates, but the paper as framed advertises convergent evidence from three independent methods and delivers one complete track plus supplementary analyses. Must reframe honestly.\n\n**[MAJOR] W3 -- Language diversity insufficient.** Primary results on EN-ZH only (CJK). EN-JA supplementary (also CJK, n=680, not significant). No Indo-European pair tested. Cannot claim \"universal\" or \"language-independent\" mechanism without typological diversity. At minimum, one EN-DE or EN-FR pair is needed.\n\n**[MAJOR] W4 -- Statistical methodology gaps.** (a) Bootstrap n=100 below standard; should be n >= 1000. (b) CKA-probe correlation r=-0.67 computed on 3 data points -- cannot be reported as a meaningful correlation. (c) MLP probe (256 units on ~1000 samples) without cross-validation or train/test gap reporting leaves overfitting concern open.\n\n**[MAJOR] W5 -- Bibliography too thin.** 10 tracked references vs 35-50 expected for ICLR. Missing critical citations: Conneau et al. (2020), Wendler et al. (2024), Wu et al. (2025 SpARE), Zhao et al. (2024 -- the NeurIPS workshop predecessor, distinct from CLEAR 2026), and recent 2025-2026 multilingual interpretability work.\n\n**[MODERATE] W6 -- H1/H2 discrimination weak.** CKA conflict/non-conflict diff = 0.08 (too small), Jaccard inconclusive (p=0.38), only probe directionality is suggestive. Framing should be \"evidence consistent with H1\" not \"H1 confirmed.\"\n\n**[MODERATE] W7 -- Probe accuracy ceiling unexplained.** 69-77% vs prior monolingual ~90% needs explicit discussion of contributing factors.\n\n**[MODERATE] W8 -- Steering non-responders uncharacterized.** At alpha=3.5, 66% of samples do not flip. No analysis of what distinguishes responders from non-responders (difficulty? confidence? entity type?).\n\n**[MODERATE] W9 -- SAE base-instruct mismatch.** Cosine similarity 0.71 between distributions renders all Track 2 results noisy. Acknowledged in the analysis but undermines cross-track validation.\n\n**[MINOR] W10 -- Jaccard statistics on small feature counts.** 8 overlapping features out of 45/42 without sensitivity analysis or null-model comparison.\n\n### Converged Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Converged | Rationale |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|-----------|\n| **Overall** | 5 | 5 | **5** | Below acceptance threshold. Strong empirical core (cross-architecture consistency) but CRITICAL baseline gap and MAJOR framing/diversity issues. |\n| **Soundness** | 3 | 2 | **2** | The missing non-conflict baseline is a soundness issue, not just a contribution issue. Statistical methodology gaps compound. |\n| **Presentation** | 3 | 3 | **3** | H1/H2 framework and experimental structure are clear. Framing overpromises. |\n| **Contribution** | 2 | 2 | **2** | Until non-conflict baseline is run, the contribution is underdetermined. If the baseline passes, contribution rises to 3. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | 4 | **4** | Both reviewers have high confidence; chair concurs given access to full experimental data. |\n\n### Recommendation: **Reject (with encouragement to resubmit)**\n\n**Rationale:** The paper addresses a well-motivated question and produces genuinely interesting results -- the cross-architecture consistency of the asymmetry effect (6.6 +/- 1.0pp, I-squared = 0%) is a real finding that deserves publication. However, the work cannot be accepted in its current form because:\n\n1. The CRITICAL non-conflict baseline gap means we cannot determine whether the central finding is conflict-specific or a general cross-lingual property. This alone is sufficient grounds for rejection.\n2. The three-track convergence framing does not match the deliverables (one complete track).\n3. Language diversity is limited to CJK, insufficient for the claims made.\n\nThese are addressable. The distance to acceptance is moderate, not large.\n\n### Prioritized Revision Plan\n\n**Priority 0 (must-do before any resubmission, ~1 week):**\n1. **Run non-conflict semantic baseline.** Probe sentiment or NLI on the same models/layers/activations. If asymmetry disappears, reframe the paper around the more general finding. If it persists at reduced magnitude, the conflict-specific component is the residual. If it's absent, the original claim stands stronger.\n2. **Increase bootstrap to n >= 1000.** Trivial compute cost; fixes CI volatility.\n\n**Priority 1 (strongly recommended, ~2-3 weeks):**\n3. **Add one non-CJK language pair.** EN-DE or EN-FR using the existing pipeline. Even n=500-700 with consistent direction would substantially strengthen universality claims.\n4. **Reframe the paper honestly.** Drop \"three-track convergence.\" Frame as: \"Cross-lingual conflict probing with layer-wise analysis and preliminary causal evidence from representation steering. SAE analysis as future work.\" This is a perfectly publishable scope for ICLR if the findings are sound.\n5. **Fix MLP probe methodology.** Report cross-validation results, train/test accuracy gap, regularization.\n6. **Expand bibliography to 35+ references.** Engage with the multilingual interpretability literature that has accumulated in 2024-2026.\n\n**Priority 2 (nice-to-have for stronger paper):**\n7. Report CKA-probe correlation as descriptive only (remove r=-0.67 inferential framing).\n8. Characterize steering non-responders vs responders.\n9. Expand dataset to n=1800 for tighter CIs.\n10. Discuss probe accuracy ceiling (69-77%) with explicit comparison to Boer et al. (2026) and Zhao et al. (2024).\n\n**Estimated time to address P0+P1:** 4-6 weeks with existing compute resources."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a8cbd1b468efc0b86 (use SendMessage with to: 'a8cbd1b468efc0b86' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 47568\ntool_uses: 11\nduration_ms: 114745</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"347907fb-725a-460a-a196-d1b396a4fd53","uuid":"c9c33bb7-eb0b-4db4-8740-cffa2a541513","timestamp":"2026-05-22T03:27:34.432Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are the **Review Chair** moderating a roundtable convergence between two independent ICLR reviewers (R1 and R2) of a mechanistic interpretability paper about cross-lingual knowledge conflict signals in multilingual LLMs.\n\n## R1 Review Summary (Methods + Experiments Focus)\n**Overall: 5/10, Soundness 3/4, Presentation 3/4, Contribution 2/4, Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Hypothesis-driven H1/H2 framework\n- Three-track convergence design\n- Cross-architecture effect size consistency (CV < 6%)\n- Thorough confound exclusion pipeline (COMET, token overlap, label noise)\n\n**Critical/Major Weaknesses:**\n1. **[CRITICAL] Missing non-conflict semantic baseline (W1):** The X→EN > EN→X asymmetry could be a general property of any cross-lingual probing task on English-dominant models, not conflict-specific. Without probing a non-conflict task (sentiment, entailment), the central contribution is underdetermined. Computationally trivial experiment.\n2. **[MAJOR] Statistical infrastructure gaps (W2):** Bootstrap n=100 is below n≥1000 standard (Efron & Tibshirani 1993), making CI endpoints volatile — consequential when Qwen's lower bound is -1.2pp. The r=-0.67 CKA-probe correlation is computed on 3-4 data points. MLP overfitting (256 units on ~1000 samples) not ruled out.\n3. **[MAJOR] Insufficient typological diversity (W3):** Both language pairs are CJK. Need at least one Indo-European pair (EN-DE or EN-FR).\n4. **[MODERATE] SAE base-instruct mismatch (W4):** Weakens cross-track validation.\n5. **[MODERATE] Probe accuracy ceiling 69-77% vs prior monolingual ~90% unexplained (W5).**\n6. **[MODERATE] Steering non-responders (65-83% of samples) uncharacterized (W7).**\n7. **[MINOR] Jaccard statistics on small feature counts without sensitivity analysis (W8).**\n\n**Questions for authors:**\n- If you probe a non-conflict semantic task (e.g., sentiment), does the X→EN > EN→X asymmetry persist?\n- Why only n=100 bootstrap iterations?\n- What is the MLP probe's held-out accuracy with proper cross-validation?\n- Can you characterize steering non-responders vs responders?\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review Summary (Novelty + Related Work Focus)\n**Overall: 5/10, Soundness 2/4, Presentation 3/4, Contribution 2/4, Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Well-motivated question (mechanistic basis for CLEAR's finding)\n- H1/H2 hypothesis structure is exemplary for interpretability work\n- Cross-architecture effect-size consistency genuinely compelling\n- Confound-exclusion pipeline thorough\n\n**Critical/Major Weaknesses:**\n1. **[CRITICAL] Novelty underdetermined:** Without non-conflict baseline, asymmetry could be a general property of English-dominant models. Single largest threat to the contribution.\n2. **[MAJOR] Two of three tracks incomplete:** Track 2 (SAE) inconclusive, Track 3 (SAE steering) absent. \"Three-track convergence\" framing overpromises. What remains is essentially a probing paper.\n3. **[MAJOR] Language diversity insufficient for \"universal\" claims:** Only CJK tested.\n4. **[MAJOR] Bibliography too thin:** 18 references vs expected 35-50 for ICLR. 10 missing references identified including Conneau et al. (2020), Zhao et al. (2024 — not the same as 2026 CLEAR), and several 2025-2026 papers on multilingual interpretability.\n5. **[MODERATE] H1-vs-H2 discrimination weak:** The evidence favors H1 but doesn't meaningfully discriminate — CKA analysis is qualitative, SAE Jaccard is inconclusive, only probe transfer direction is suggestive.\n6. **[MODERATE] \"First mechanistic account\" claim needs qualification — Wendler et al. 2024 and SpARE (Wu et al. 2025) overlap.\n\n**Path to acceptance (R2's view):** Add non-conflict baseline + one non-CJK language pair + fix SAE mismatch OR reframe as a probing paper. Any two of three would likely suffice.\n\n---\n\n## Your Task: Moderate Convergence\n\nSimulate Step 2 (cross-sharing) and Step 3 (convergence) of the roundtable:\n\n1. **Identify consensus points** — what do both reviewers agree on?\n2. **Identify disagreements** — where do they differ in severity or interpretation?\n3. **Resolve disagreements** — as chair, adjudicate using the evidence\n4. **Produce a merged assessment** with:\n   - Consolidated strengths (merged, deduplicated)\n   - Consolidated weaknesses (ranked by severity, with [CRITICAL/MAJOR/MODERATE/MINOR] tags)\n   - Converged overall score and per-dimension scores\n   - A clear accept/reject recommendation with rationale\n   - Actionable revision suggestions prioritized by impact\n\nUse ICLR scale: Overall {1, 3, 5, 6, 8, 10}, Soundness/Presentation/Contribution 1-4, Confidence 1-5.\n\nBe thorough but concise. The output should be the final converged roundtable result ready for inclusion in the review report.","agentId":"a8cbd1b468efc0b86","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the actual project state, experimental results, and the paper framing. Let me produce the converged roundtable assessment.\n\n---\n\n# ICLR Review Roundtable -- Converged Assessment\n\n**Paper:** Mechanistic Interpretability of Cross-Lingual Knowledge Conflict Signals in Multilingual LLMs\n**Reviewers:** R1 (Methods/Experiments), R2 (Novelty/Related Work)\n**Chair:** Review Chair\n\n---\n\n## 1. Consensus Points\n\nBoth reviewers agree on the following without dispute:\n\n- **The research question is well-motivated.** Providing a mechanistic account of CLEAR's linguistic-affinity finding fills a genuine gap.\n- **The H1/H2 hypothesis framework is a strength.** Pre-registered competing hypotheses are exemplary for interpretability work.\n- **Cross-architecture effect-size consistency is genuinely compelling.** The CV < 6% across three architectures (Llama 7.22pp, Qwen 7.18pp, Gemma 5.4pp; pooled 6.60 +/- 1.04pp) with I-squared = 0% is the paper's strongest empirical asset.\n- **The confound-exclusion pipeline is thorough.** COMET tercile ablation (reverse direction, p=0.695), token-overlap Jaccard analysis (3-4%, negative correlation in Llama), and label-noise ablation collectively address multiple plausible confounds.\n- **[CRITICAL] The non-conflict semantic baseline is missing.** Both reviewers independently identified this as the single largest threat. The X-to-EN > EN-to-X asymmetry could reflect a general property of cross-lingual probing on English-dominant models rather than anything conflict-specific. This is computationally trivial to run (sentiment/entailment probing on the same activations).\n- **Language diversity is insufficient for current claims.** Only CJK languages tested (EN-ZH primary, EN-JA supplementary with n=680). No Indo-European pair.\n- **Track 2 (SAE) is inconclusive and Track 3 (SAE steering) is absent.** The \"three-track convergence\" framing overpromises relative to deliverables.\n- **Bootstrap n=100 is below standard.** Both reviewers flag this, though with different emphasis.\n\n## 2. Disagreements and Chair Adjudication\n\n### 2a. Severity of the non-conflict baseline gap\n\n- **R1:** [CRITICAL] -- computationally trivial, underdetermines the central contribution.\n- **R2:** [CRITICAL] -- single largest threat to novelty.\n- **Chair ruling:** Both assign CRITICAL. **Unanimous. Retained as CRITICAL.** I note from the project data that the probe-based steering experiment (34% flip rate vs 6% random, Cohen's d = 0.41) provides indirect evidence that the signal is conflict-related (random directions don't produce similar flips), but this does not substitute for the direct baseline experiment. The non-conflict baseline remains the most important missing piece.\n\n### 2b. Framing: \"Probing paper\" vs \"Three-track convergence\"\n\n- **R1:** Flags Tracks 2-3 as moderate concerns (W4, W7) but evaluates the probing results on their own merits.\n- **R2:** Elevates this to MAJOR -- the framing overpromises, and what remains is \"essentially a probing paper.\"\n- **Chair ruling:** **R2 is correct in substance but overweights the framing issue.** The dream simulation data confirms: Track 2 (SAE) produced noisy results due to base-instruct mismatch (cosine similarity 0.71), Track 3 was not executed. However, the probe-based representation steering (plan_107) partially fills the causal gap with a 34% flip rate vs 6% random control. The paper has (1) probing, (2) CKA/SVCCA layer analysis, (3) probe-weight steering -- three distinct analytical approaches, even if not the originally advertised three SAE-based tracks. **Severity: MAJOR.** The title/framing must be revised, but the underlying work has more substance than \"just probing.\"\n\n### 2c. Statistical infrastructure (bootstrap, CKA correlation, MLP overfitting)\n\n- **R1:** [MAJOR] -- bootstrap n=100, CKA-probe r=-0.67 on 3-4 points, MLP overfitting uncontrolled.\n- **R2:** Mentions bootstrap tangentially but does not flag CKA or MLP issues.\n- **Chair ruling:** **Partially agree with R1.** Bootstrap n=100 is indeed below the Efron & Tibshirani standard. However, the dream simulation shows that expanding to n=1800 samples tightens CIs substantially (Llama: [1.1, 13.6] to [2.3, 11.7]), and the expanded dataset is planned. The CKA-probe correlation (r=-0.67 on 3 models) is indeed underpowered -- this should be reported as descriptive, not inferential. MLP overfitting concern is valid: 256-unit MLP on ~1000 samples without reported cross-validation is a gap. **Severity: MAJOR (aggregated), but each sub-issue is individually MODERATE.**\n\n### 2d. Bibliography depth\n\n- **R1:** Does not flag.\n- **R2:** [MAJOR] -- 18 references vs expected 35-50, names 10 missing papers.\n- **Chair ruling:** **R2 is correct.** The literature_map.yaml shows only 10 tracked papers. For an ICLR submission claiming \"first mechanistic account,\" the related work must engage with Conneau et al. (2020) XLM-R probing, Wendler et al. (2024) on language-agnostic representations, Wu et al. (2025) SpARE, and the broader multilingual interpretability literature. **Severity: MAJOR** for submission readiness, but **easily fixable** -- this is writing work, not experimental work.\n\n### 2e. H1-vs-H2 discrimination\n\n- **R1:** Not explicitly flagged as a standalone weakness.\n- **R2:** [MODERATE] -- evidence favors H1 but doesn't meaningfully discriminate.\n- **Chair ruling:** **R2 is correct.** The CKA analysis is qualitative (conflict vs non-conflict CKA diff = 0.08, too small for strong claims). The Jaccard analysis is inconclusive (0.06 vs 0.04, p=0.38). Only the probe transfer directionality (ZH-to-EN > EN-to-ZH consistently) is suggestive of H1. The paper should honestly frame this as \"evidence consistent with H1\" rather than \"discrimination between H1 and H2.\" **Severity: MODERATE.**\n\n### 2f. Probe accuracy ceiling (69-77% vs monolingual ~90%)\n\n- **R1:** [MODERATE] -- unexplained gap vs Zhao et al. 2024.\n- **R2:** Not flagged.\n- **Chair ruling:** **Valid concern.** Boer et al. (2026) achieve 0.96 F1 monolingually; Zhao et al. (2024) reach ~90%. The paper's 69-77% needs explanation. Plausible factors: cross-lingual setting inherently harder, ConflictQA vs synthetic data, linear vs MLP probe, balanced accuracy vs F1 metric. The label-noise ablation (estimated 4% pipeline error causing only ~1pp degradation) rules out data quality. **Severity: MODERATE.** Must be discussed, but not fatal -- the gap likely reflects genuine difficulty of cross-lingual conflict detection.\n\n## 3. Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Strengths (merged, deduplicated)\n\n1. **Well-motivated mechanistic question** bridging CLEAR's behavioral findings to internal representations, with clear hypothesis-driven structure (H1/H2).\n2. **Cross-architecture consistency** of the asymmetry effect (6.60 +/- 1.04pp across Llama, Qwen, Gemma; I-squared = 0%; Cochran's Q p=0.68) is the paper's most compelling result.\n3. **Rigorous confound exclusion**: COMET translation-quality ablation (reverse-direction effect rules out quality confound), token-overlap analysis (Jaccard 3-4%, negative correlation), and label-noise robustness testing (4% error rate causes < 1pp degradation).\n4. **Probe-weight steering** provides preliminary causal evidence beyond pure probing (34% flip rate vs 6% random, d=0.41), with informative alpha-sensitivity curve showing coherence-effectiveness tradeoff.\n5. **CKA/SVCCA layer analysis** connects conflict signal peaks to the language-transition window identified in prior work (Wang et al. 2025), providing mechanistic narrative.\n\n### Weaknesses (ranked by severity)\n\n**[CRITICAL] W1 -- Missing non-conflict semantic baseline.** The central claim (asymmetry is conflict-specific) is underdetermined without probing a non-conflict cross-lingual task on the same models and activations. If sentiment or NLI probing shows the same X-to-EN > EN-to-X pattern, the paper's contribution collapses to documenting a general cross-lingual probing asymmetry. This experiment is computationally trivial (hours, not days).\n\n**[MAJOR] W2 -- Three-track framing overpromises.** Track 2 (SAE) is inconclusive due to base-instruct mismatch. Track 3 (SAE steering) was not executed. The probe-weight steering partially compensates, but the paper as framed advertises convergent evidence from three independent methods and delivers one complete track plus supplementary analyses. Must reframe honestly.\n\n**[MAJOR] W3 -- Language diversity insufficient.** Primary results on EN-ZH only (CJK). EN-JA supplementary (also CJK, n=680, not significant). No Indo-European pair tested. Cannot claim \"universal\" or \"language-independent\" mechanism without typological diversity. At minimum, one EN-DE or EN-FR pair is needed.\n\n**[MAJOR] W4 -- Statistical methodology gaps.** (a) Bootstrap n=100 below standard; should be n >= 1000. (b) CKA-probe correlation r=-0.67 computed on 3 data points -- cannot be reported as a meaningful correlation. (c) MLP probe (256 units on ~1000 samples) without cross-validation or train/test gap reporting leaves overfitting concern open.\n\n**[MAJOR] W5 -- Bibliography too thin.** 10 tracked references vs 35-50 expected for ICLR. Missing critical citations: Conneau et al. (2020), Wendler et al. (2024), Wu et al. (2025 SpARE), Zhao et al. (2024 -- the NeurIPS workshop predecessor, distinct from CLEAR 2026), and recent 2025-2026 multilingual interpretability work.\n\n**[MODERATE] W6 -- H1/H2 discrimination weak.** CKA conflict/non-conflict diff = 0.08 (too small), Jaccard inconclusive (p=0.38), only probe directionality is suggestive. Framing should be \"evidence consistent with H1\" not \"H1 confirmed.\"\n\n**[MODERATE] W7 -- Probe accuracy ceiling unexplained.** 69-77% vs prior monolingual ~90% needs explicit discussion of contributing factors.\n\n**[MODERATE] W8 -- Steering non-responders uncharacterized.** At alpha=3.5, 66% of samples do not flip. No analysis of what distinguishes responders from non-responders (difficulty? confidence? entity type?).\n\n**[MODERATE] W9 -- SAE base-instruct mismatch.** Cosine similarity 0.71 between distributions renders all Track 2 results noisy. Acknowledged in the analysis but undermines cross-track validation.\n\n**[MINOR] W10 -- Jaccard statistics on small feature counts.** 8 overlapping features out of 45/42 without sensitivity analysis or null-model comparison.\n\n### Converged Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Converged | Rationale |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|-----------|\n| **Overall** | 5 | 5 | **5** | Below acceptance threshold. Strong empirical core (cross-architecture consistency) but CRITICAL baseline gap and MAJOR framing/diversity issues. |\n| **Soundness** | 3 | 2 | **2** | The missing non-conflict baseline is a soundness issue, not just a contribution issue. Statistical methodology gaps compound. |\n| **Presentation** | 3 | 3 | **3** | H1/H2 framework and experimental structure are clear. Framing overpromises. |\n| **Contribution** | 2 | 2 | **2** | Until non-conflict baseline is run, the contribution is underdetermined. If the baseline passes, contribution rises to 3. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | 4 | **4** | Both reviewers have high confidence; chair concurs given access to full experimental data. |\n\n### Recommendation: **Reject (with encouragement to resubmit)**\n\n**Rationale:** The paper addresses a well-motivated question and produces genuinely interesting results -- the cross-architecture consistency of the asymmetry effect (6.6 +/- 1.0pp, I-squared = 0%) is a real finding that deserves publication. However, the work cannot be accepted in its current form because:\n\n1. The CRITICAL non-conflict baseline gap means we cannot determine whether the central finding is conflict-specific or a general cross-lingual property. This alone is sufficient grounds for rejection.\n2. The three-track convergence framing does not match the deliverables (one complete track).\n3. Language diversity is limited to CJK, insufficient for the claims made.\n\nThese are addressable. The distance to acceptance is moderate, not large.\n\n### Prioritized Revision Plan\n\n**Priority 0 (must-do before any resubmission, ~1 week):**\n1. **Run non-conflict semantic baseline.** Probe sentiment or NLI on the same models/layers/activations. If asymmetry disappears, reframe the paper around the more general finding. If it persists at reduced magnitude, the conflict-specific component is the residual. If it's absent, the original claim stands stronger.\n2. **Increase bootstrap to n >= 1000.** Trivial compute cost; fixes CI volatility.\n\n**Priority 1 (strongly recommended, ~2-3 weeks):**\n3. **Add one non-CJK language pair.** EN-DE or EN-FR using the existing pipeline. Even n=500-700 with consistent direction would substantially strengthen universality claims.\n4. **Reframe the paper honestly.** Drop \"three-track convergence.\" Frame as: \"Cross-lingual conflict probing with layer-wise analysis and preliminary causal evidence from representation steering. SAE analysis as future work.\" This is a perfectly publishable scope for ICLR if the findings are sound.\n5. **Fix MLP probe methodology.** Report cross-validation results, train/test accuracy gap, regularization.\n6. **Expand bibliography to 35+ references.** Engage with the multilingual interpretability literature that has accumulated in 2024-2026.\n\n**Priority 2 (nice-to-have for stronger paper):**\n7. Report CKA-probe correlation as descriptive only (remove r=-0.67 inferential framing).\n8. Characterize steering non-responders vs responders.\n9. Expand dataset to n=1800 for tighter CIs.\n10. Discuss probe accuracy ceiling (69-77%) with explicit comparison to Boer et al. (2026) and Zhao et al. (2024).\n\n**Estimated time to address P0+P1:** 4-6 weeks with existing compute resources."}],"totalDurationMs":114745,"totalTokens":47568,"totalToolUseCount":11,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14056,"cache_read_input_tokens":29679,"output_tokens":3832,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14056},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3832,"cache_read_input_tokens":29679,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14056,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14056,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
